<h2 class="sec">The short answer</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>There are three control schemes:</b> Default, Classic and Left-Handed. You can pick one at first launch and change it any time in Settings.</li>
  <li><b>You can't freely remap buttons in the game.</b> Rockstar's list doesn't include it, and the Centre for Accessibility Australia says the controls can't be remapped in game, so it points players to the PS5 and Xbox system settings. On PS5 that's a console feature, and the steps are below.</li>
  <li><b>The accessibility list is big:</b> colorblind modes, adjustable subtitles with speaker names, accessible combat, Auto Drive, simplified minigames, high contrast, camera shake controls, mono audio, menu narration and audio descriptions.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="sec">Default vs Classic vs Left-Handed</h2>
<p>If your GTA 5 muscle memory fights the new layout, Classic is built for you. GamesRadar says to press the Options/Menu button, go to Settings and switch the scheme there. Per GamesRadar, in the Default scheme running is on L3 (the left stick click) and X/A is jump, with melee on Square/X. Classic changes four things:</p>
<table class="guide-tbl"><thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Default</th><th>Classic</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Run / sprint</td><td>L3 (left stick click)</td><td>X / A</td></tr>
<tr><td>Jump / climb</td><td>X / A</td><td>Square / X</td></tr>
<tr><td>Melee</td><td>Square / X</td><td>Circle / B</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crouch</td><td>Not listed by GamesRadar</td><td>L3</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p class="small">Table based on GamesRadar's Classic list and its description of the Default scheme. PlayStation button names first, then Xbox. I don't have a sourced list for the Left-Handed layout. Rockstar names it as a scheme but its article doesn't spell out the layout, so check the in-game control screen. Sources: <a href="https://www.gamesradar.com/games/grand-theft-auto/gta-6-classic-controls/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">GamesRadar</a>, <a href={RS} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Rockstar Support</a>.</p>
<h3>The dynamic HUD</h3>
<p>GamesRadar says the standard Dynamic HUD hides non-essential info, so you press down on the d-pad to bring up things like the minimap, bank balances, and the health, stamina and focus bars. Each of those can be toggled in Settings, and Rockstar lists a large number of granular HUD visibility settings. Want the camera options too? Those are on our <a href="/gta-6/tips/#settings">tips page</a>.</p>

<h2 class="sec">How to remap buttons on PS5</h2>
<p>Since the game doesn't have free remapping, PlayStation's own steps are the way to go. From PlayStation Support: go to <b>Settings, Accessibility</b>, select <b>Controllers</b>, then <b>Custom Button Assignments for DualSense Wireless Controller</b>. Select the button you want to change, pick the function to assign, then select <b>Apply</b>. Xbox has its own system-level options too, but I only sourced PlayStation's steps, so check Microsoft's support pages for those. Source: <a href="https://www.playstation.com/en-us/support/hardware/ps5-accessibility-settings/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">PlayStation Support</a>.</p>

<h2 class="sec">Rockstar's accessibility options</h2>
<p>Rockstar's support article sorts the options into six groups. They can all be set at first launch and changed later in Settings. Here's the list, in plain language, straight from <a href={RS} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Rockstar Support</a>.</p>
{groups.map(([name, items]) => <>
  <h3>{name}</h3>
  <ul>{items.map(i => <li>{i}</li>)}</ul>
</>)}
<div class="callout cyan">
  <p><b>Good to know from an outside review.</b> The Centre for Accessibility Australia says the game asks at first launch whether you want the built-in screen reader, and that the Accessibility section sits in the Gameplay tab with 15 options. It also says the screen reader only works in the pause menu and on the map and story progress screens, and doesn't read out landmark info on the map. They call it good but limited in practice. They conclude Rockstar "put effort into making this game accessible." Source: <a href="https://www.accessibility.org.au/grand-theft-auto-vi-accessibility-review-what-works-and-what-needs-improving/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Centre for Accessibility Australia</a>.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="sec">PS5 system options that stack with the game's</h2>
<p>Your console has its own accessibility settings under Settings, Accessibility. PlayStation lists Zoom, a color filter under Display and Sound, closed captions, a screen reader and Mono Audio for headphones among them. PlayStation's store page for the game also lists the accessibility features it declares for GTA 6, and you can view those from the game's hub in the PlayStation Store. Source: <a href="https://www.playstation.com/en-us/support/hardware/ps5-accessibility-settings/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">PlayStation Support</a>.</p>

<h2 class="sec">PC?</h2>
<p>Rockstar hasn't announced a PC version of GTA 6, so these options are for PS5 and Xbox Series X|S only.</p>

